`karr edit 1 --block "waiting"` on a card bob holds answers

    Task 1 is claimed by bob
    1 of 1 ids failed

and stops. kanban-md says at the same spot: `task #1 is claimed by "bob" (expires in 59m). If this is you, add: --claim bob`. karr's line is bare on purpose: the comment above the claim check in Role::ClaimTimeout explains that `karr delete` has no `--claim` option, so a `--claim X` hint would be unfollowable for one of its callers (move, edit, handoff, delete), and the wording was left as `karr handoff` always had it.

That is a workaround for a gap, not a resolution. Today the only way to delete a claimed card is `karr edit 1 --release` first (which needs no claim, see below), then `karr delete 1 --yes`, and nothing tells the caller so.

## What to build

1. `karr delete --claim NAME`, and `karr archive --claim NAME` while there (kanban-md's archive takes it; the guard in Role::ClaimTimeout is shared).
2. Then the refusal ends in the working command in k263's shape: built from the caller's own words, real id, last line:

       Task 1 is claimed by bob:
         karr edit 1 --block waiting --claim bob

   For a caller that is not bob the honest line is the release, `karr edit 1 --release`; say that too (both lines, or a message that names both).
3. Note the asymmetry while there: `karr edit 1 --release` succeeds with no `--claim` at all, and `karr edit 1 --claim carol` silently takes the card off bob. Whether release/steal stay unguarded is a deliberate choice (kanban-md is the same); record it in the POD of the claim check so the next reader does not file it as a bug. But the refusal must not pretend the card is unreachable when it is a release away.

Cover move, edit, handoff, delete and archive in t/. Exit codes unchanged. Changes entry.

Pointers: the check is the die 'Task %d is claimed by %s' in Role::ClaimTimeout, with the long comment above it that explains the bare wording; the suggestion renderer is App::karr::Error/command_hint with claim_hint_tokens on Role::TaskMutation (k263), and the batch runner keeps the hint through clean_error. Acceptance: karr delete 1 --yes on a card bob holds ends on the line karr delete 1 --yes --claim bob; karr edit 1 --block waiting ends on karr edit 1 --block waiting --claim bob; both also name karr edit 1 --release as the other way out; exit codes stay 1, --json keeps its single-line error field.

